<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Phocaea</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 BCE – 133 BCE (approx.) · capital Phocaea</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ionian city whose daring sailors founded Massalia and explored the western Mediterranean as far as Tartessos in Spain.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Phocaea, the northernmost Ionian city, was founded on a peninsula north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediz-river">Hermus</a> c. 1000 BCE by settlers said to have come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocis">Phocis</a>. Its rocky territory pushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocaeans">Phocaeans</a> to the sea, and from the 7th century BCE they sailed long distances in fast fifty-oared ships.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> credits <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocaeans">Phocaeans</a> with opening the Adriatic, Tyrrhenian and Iberian seas. They founded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lampsacus">Lampsacus</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/massalia">Massalia</a> c. 600 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alalia">Alalia</a> on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/corsican-republic">Corsica</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emporion">Emporion</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and won the friendship of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arganthonios">Arganthonius</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tartessos">Tartessos</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harpagus">Harpagus</a> besieged the city c. 545 BCE, most citizens sailed west; those at Alalia fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a> c. 540 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Phocaea recovered and provided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysius-of-phocaea">Dionysius</a>, commander of the Greek fleet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lade">Lade</a> in 494 BCE. In 132 BCE it backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristonicus">Aristonicus</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and was spared destruction only through Massalia's appeal, then joined the province of Asia.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/phocaea.jpg" alt="Phocaea" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: QuartierLatin1968 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_theatre_Fo%C3%A7a525.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 BCE – 133 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Phocaea</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ionic Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Phokaia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-566&amp;focus=phocaea" aria-label="Phocaea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5344 790 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Phocaea lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5664 1046L6140 1251M5664 1046L5591 1068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6140 1251h0M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5664 1046h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5664 1046h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-566&amp;focus=phocaea">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

